WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: 

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.740
Imagine walking five kilometers
to school every day,

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.300
under the scorching sun,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.820
no trees, no shed in sight,

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.700
writing final exams outside
in the dry, sweltering heat,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.420
and still being expected
to pass with flying colors.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.180
And then after school,
or sometimes before,

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.380
or sometimes both,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.140
trekking four hours
to the nearest water source,

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.500
standing in long queues

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.260
just to get a few drops
from a drying well.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.300
This is the daily life of myself

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.780
and over thousands
of schoolchildren in my city alone.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.700
And the life of millions of children
all across Africa and around the globe.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.420
Climate change is making our lives harder,

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.580
sometimes unbearably so.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.140
The injustice, however,

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.540
is that climate change is mostly caused
by adults in developed nations.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:55.820
And yet it mostly hurts
children in developing countries,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.420
especially when you think
about our dwindling future.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.300
We are the ones with the most at stake,

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.540
which is why we need to have
a seat at the table for fighting

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.180
for climate justice solutions.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.580
My name is Nkosilathi Nyathi,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:13.260
I'm UNICEF Youth Climate Advocate
and a proud Zimbabwean.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.260
I live in a town called Victoria Falls,

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.100
which is home to one of the seven
wonders of the world.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.500
Here we call it Mosi-oa-Tunya,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.500
and this is Tonga
for “the smoke that thunders.”

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.180
I grew up in Chinotimba Township,
a poor part of our region,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.780
where many are struggling.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.860
But as a child, I noticed on top
of existing economic challenges,

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.740
we started having new challenges
caused by environmental impact.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.740
For example, I started noticing
the unpredictability

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.060
and uncertainty of weather patterns.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.860
Sometimes we're going to have
excessive rains and floods

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.900
that could wash away our top soil,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.260
which we depend on for agriculture.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.180
The water would drown our animals,

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:01.460
destroy our farmers’ hard-earned crops
right before the harvest.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.900
And then sometimes,

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.460
the weather patterns were the opposite.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.260
We would experience periods
of extreme droughts, no rainfall.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.260
The land dry and cracking,

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.140
our crops were ruined,
and our animals died then, too.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.100
In 2017, we were warned
by the local government

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.420
not to go to school

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.260
because Cyclone Dineo
was to hit my region.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.020
I read my books in fear that day.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.900
From that one tropical storm alone,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.460
20,000 homes were destroyed,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:37.540
and 130,000 people were displaced
in Zimbabwe and Mozambique.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.140
I asked myself:

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:44.020
What crime has my generation
committed to deserve this?

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.220
Then, just two years later,

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.500
Cyclone Idai hit, devastating
the eastern part of my country.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.780
It claimed over 1,000 lives,

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.300
affecting another
three million in the region.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.900
Schools and homes were destroyed.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.500
Over 9,000 students
had their schooling disrupted.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.860
What wrong have we done to deserve this?

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.500
It's hard to be a child no matter what,

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.140
but especially hard to have
your childhood torn apart

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.060
by climate disaster
after climate disaster,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.220
and wonder what the future holds.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.940
The present is already such a struggle.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.940
Why continue fighting
if the future will bring more destruction?

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:26.100
This is a crisis my generation faces,

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:31.220
and this is why I became an activist
to fight to even have a future.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.380
I decided to ask the questions

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.620
most people my age were not asking.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:37.500
Why is this happening,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.020
or who is to blame, and what is to blame?

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.100
Is there anything we can do to stop it?

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.260
To stop the interruptions
to our schooling,

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:45.540
to our livelihoods,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.900
to stop the fear we have for the future.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.980
Since I was 10, I started
to recognize all these changes.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.420
I became passionate about climate change
and the environment.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.940
I joined environmental club
since grade five

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:02.180
and I'm currently the president
of the environmental club at my school,

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.940
Inyathi High School.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.220
When I was 12, I led the initiation
of a functional biogas plant.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:11.140
I learned that if we composted food scraps

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.300
in a special container,
they would break down

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.940
and provide natural gas

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.140
that could be tabbed on
into a stove to cook on.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.940
The project is the first
of its kind in Victoria Falls,

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:26.300
and other people have also implemented
the initiative in their home states.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:31.940
Now students can learn about something
which they can see in their premises.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:37.300
It does not only reuse our food
and kitchen waste in a sustainable way,

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.740
but it means we don't need to rely
on burning fossil fuels

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.300
or wood for energy.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.660
And in a place like Zimbabwe,

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.820
where we have a terrible
fuel and power crisis,

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.020
this helped us survive.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.540
From the dusty grounds of my native land

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.580
to the blinding lights
of Madrid and Milan,

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.140
my activism has led me
to major climate change events

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:04.820
representing the voices
of young people demanding change.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.500
But every time I hear
the climate activists speak on a podium,

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.460
they would point out how we,
young people, are not included

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.420
in decision making process.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.380
Something I noted myself
back home in Zimbabwe,

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.540
where there are more opportunities
for young people to join events

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.780
and speak to important decision makers.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:26.500
Now it is the right time to truly
and meaningfully listen to our ideas

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.620
and include us in these decisions.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.380
I tell you,

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.780
my concerned generation
has more to offer than ever before.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.300
This is because we live climate change

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.060
in a way our parents' generation did not.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.900
Think of someone in a remote
and marginalized place

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.500
who can't afford an umbrella,

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.900
but has to walk to school in the rain

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.700
and is expected to pass and excel.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.820
Climate change is a child right crisis.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:58.020
It is also children in remote areas
in developing countries

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.420
who have contributed less
to the brink of this catastrophe.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:05.180
Teach us more about climate
change as we live it.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.460
Include it in our subjects,
in our school curriculums at all levels.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.580
If we as a generation are more informed,

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:15.980
we will have more to offer
in this crucial period in humanity

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.620
to solve the climate crisis.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.420
It will be upon us.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:24.660
When I was 15, Victoria Falls suffered
its greatest drought in a century.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.460
The mighty waters became,
almost overnight, barely a trickle

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:31.420
beyond just the usual seasonal changes.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:33.820
At the same time this was happening,

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.620
more water shortages
were befalling all of Africa,

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:40.860
and 45 million of my fellow Africans
were suffering food insecurity

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.540
because of crop failures.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.820
These kinds of tragedies
should never happen.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:47.860
Not once during childhood.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.020
Not ever.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.700
They cannot be what looms on our horizon.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.900
We can fight,

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:57.140
we can heal our planet
with accountable, responsible solutions,

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:01.260
but only if we include young people
who have the most at stake.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.700
Because thankfully,
the waterfall still rains down.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.260
But we, young people
are the Mosi-oa-Tunya.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.980
We are “the smoke that thunders.”

